  'Asian History',
  'Asian History',
  'Asian History is an advanced undergraduate course that introduces students to the major political, social, cultural, religious, economic, imperial, and environmental histories of Asia from ancient foundations to the present. The course treats Asia as a connected but internally diverse field, integrating East Asia, South Asia, Southeast Asia, Central and Inner Asia, and Islamic and Indian Ocean worlds. Students work with primary sources, material culture, maps, visual evidence, historiography, and digital archives while building a final source-based historical argument.',
  $asiajson${"slug": "asian-history", "title": "Asian History", "description": "Asian History is an advanced undergraduate course that introduces students to the major political, social, cultural, religious, economic, imperial, and environmental histories of Asia from ancient foundations to the present. The course treats Asia as a connected but internally diverse field, integrating East Asia, South Asia, Southeast Asia, Central and Inner Asia, and Islamic and Indian Ocean worlds. Students work with primary sources, material culture, maps, visual evidence, historiography, and digital archives while building a final source-based historical argument.", "topics": [{"id": "bf579dab-bcb7-4252-aca8-7d81250cb2b6", "type": "topic", "title": "Framing Asian History: Regions, Scales, Sources, and Historiography", "description": "This opening section gives students the intellectual map for the course. The point is to avoid treating Asia as a single civilization or a backdrop for Western expansion, and instead to ask how historians construct regions, periods, archives, and arguments.", "children": [{"id": "1f8ee1ec-7046-4e2f-ade3-6fadd56a2a0f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Asia as a historical category", "children": [{"id": "9e32dc31-db71-42d8-a36b-7917b6856782", "type": "concept", "title": "Area studies and its critiques"}, {"id": "effdbaaf-3d46-43a2-a0ad-1c8f43655918", "type": "concept", "title": "Continental, maritime, and borderland perspectives"}, {"id": "96d4e0d7-64a5-4a7e-b5e9-95650a32417b", "type": "concept", "title": "Avoiding civilizational essentialism"}], "description": "Students learn why East, South, Southeast, Central, and West Asia are useful but imperfect analytical labels."}, {"id": "e55863af-e1c9-4634-b076-d5a24f42efe1", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Historical scale and comparison", "children": [{"id": "78be1e9d-07dc-4f47-b7de-f63c5fb29eea", "type": "concept", "title": "Local case studies"}, {"id": "0073ab06-8d73-40f4-9eb3-0e81e2b867e9", "type": "concept", "title": "Regional comparison"}, {"id": "56a77066-e0f8-47d6-a031-e4d333b1f29a", "type": "concept", "title": "Transnational and connected histories"}], "description": "The gist is to move confidently between village, dynasty, empire, ocean, diaspora, and global system."}, {"id": "20a01d51-e29f-4175-afd3-9dd6cbb25f83", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Sources and archives", "children": [{"id": "989a7dec-143c-4833-80b9-c241ec4490a7", "type": "concept", "title": "Language and translation"}, {"id": "ed1934bb-d315-42a1-a8f8-32763ee72abe", "type": "concept", "title": "Material culture"}, {"id": "2ffddde3-3b8b-4548-9524-1efbdf0910d8", "type": "concept", "title": "Colonial archives and archival silences"}], "description": "Students see how texts, inscriptions, objects, images, maps, oral histories, and state records produce different kinds of evidence."}, {"id": "07f91800-832c-46df-b531-d5f64370cb83", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Historiographic debates", "children": [{"id": "057addfb-737d-4e48-9c92-cf9cc54d8cfe", "type": "concept", "title": "Orientalism and area studies"}, {"id": "870e7151-1e49-48ce-a0bb-65e389c6fb4e", "type": "concept", "title": "Nationalist historiography"}, {"id": "3ed07e68-1035-4d33-b2da-85083545f975", "type": "concept", "title": "Subaltern and postcolonial approaches"}], "description": "This introduces the big interpretive disagreements that will recur throughout the course."}]}, {"id": "861b90c7-f93b-47d1-849a-1c5227dbe9b2", "type": "topic", "title": "Ancient and Classical Foundations: South, East, Southeast, and Central Asia", "description": "This section establishes the deep structures that later states and societies inherited, contested, and reworked. Students focus on political formation, writing, social hierarchy, and exchange rather than memorizing dynasties in isolation.", "children": [{"id": "1c14e279-b4c5-4af2-823a-6865ec4ca46b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Early urbanism and states", "children": [{"id": "1fb24917-eb55-49c7-ace1-d15ba29167a2", "type": "concept", "title": "Indus Valley"}, {"id": "4dec8463-e20e-4788-8d3f-ff8cf0c70110", "type": "concept", "title": "Shang and Zhou China"}, {"id": "a53cfaff-3fc4-4b82-9109-cab1b50847f7", "type": "concept", "title": "Early Southeast Asian polities"}], "description": "The point is to compare how early complex societies organized power, labor, ritual, and space."}, {"id": "7b3f38b3-59c2-46c5-8de2-8629ccbc8bea", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Classical political cultures", "children": [{"id": "a6665415-2888-4411-92bf-2389e4875dc0", "type": "concept", "title": "Mauryan and Gupta worlds"}, {"id": "eb6a0ebe-4e56-411b-9309-71f0cf68dd52", "type": "concept", "title": "Qin-Han statecraft"}, {"id": "ac18b490-33b9-47ea-9195-f6772d112936", "type": "concept", "title": "Mandate, bureaucracy, and law"}], "description": "Students learn how rulers justified authority and how institutions shaped social order."}, {"id": "be9a9fe0-8a1f-495d-a2eb-bbdbf3112492", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Writing, memory, and recordkeeping", "children": [{"id": "d6994224-483f-4555-bfb9-d8ae09d61a4a", "type": "concept", "title": "Inscriptions"}, {"id": "13456b96-e399-4394-a28d-ebdf0f41d7a6", "type": "concept", "title": "Chronicles and court histories"}, {"id": "9bc7340b-a288-4d52-aa0d-d6bf7fafb0c3", "type": "concept", "title": "Archaeological evidence"}], "description": "This subtopic shows how historical knowledge depends on durable media and elite preservation."}, {"id": "8778c940-ef19-40b1-965f-c60c89f8516b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Exchange before empire", "children": [{"id": "5fd25202-1538-419c-9ed4-03b51aed7736", "type": "concept", "title": "Silk Roads"}, {"id": "271fb9eb-885a-4d92-87ab-5d62314a1c00", "type": "concept", "title": "Indian Ocean exchange"}, {"id": "ef2fe401-eeee-4101-956f-8cdb30b33669", "type": "concept", "title": "Steppe-sedentary interaction"}], "description": "Students trace early movement of goods, peoples, and ideas across land and sea."}]}, {"id": "4bb8ce50-99db-4578-9da8-338b69279723", "type": "topic", "title": "Religions, Ideas, and Cultural Worlds", "description": "Religion and thought are treated here as institutions, ethical systems, social practices, and transregional networks. Students learn to connect belief, patronage, law, art, pilgrimage, and political legitimacy.", "children": [{"id": "d2f85aae-026e-487f-bac5-dc472cb2ac48", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Buddhist circulation", "children": [{"id": "dfd0b57f-b027-43db-9bc0-556c419416bf", "type": "concept", "title": "South Asian origins"}, {"id": "a12236aa-1c9b-4b3d-84d9-3302488f89a4", "type": "concept", "title": "Central Asian transmission"}, {"id": "bf0b75fd-6eac-4c7f-a5ee-b9c0abdbcb4f", "type": "concept", "title": "China, Korea, Japan, and Southeast Asia"}], "description": "Students examine Buddhism as a mobile tradition shaped by translation, monastic networks, patronage, and local adaptation."}, {"id": "efeadd04-88dc-4e07-8f7d-2549865061c3", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Confucian, Daoist, and legal traditions", "children": [{"id": "117dd49c-c041-4ee4-aa90-e4218b0695aa", "type": "concept", "title": "Classics and education"}, {"id": "d907043b-c030-407b-a744-4660b4ed86c7", "type": "concept", "title": "Ritual and hierarchy"}, {"id": "34279496-8a36-4ef2-94a7-2101f8508e66", "type": "concept", "title": "State orthodoxy and dissent"}], "description": "The gist is to see East Asian governance and family order as historically produced and debated."}, {"id": "670e8006-1b6a-4060-8e1d-827b05d724bf", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Islamic worlds in Asia", "children": [{"id": "a9a91330-3f9a-49e4-8d99-a54f93c9df16", "type": "concept", "title": "Caliphates and ulama"}, {"id": "1a5e8d66-7672-4fdf-b612-eb5b91a48c20", "type": "concept", "title": "Sufi networks"}, {"id": "17a0a119-b432-4133-b77b-603f86b41f92", "type": "concept", "title": "Islam in South and Southeast Asia"}], "description": "Students learn how Islam linked West, Central, South, and Southeast Asia through trade, law, scholarship, and empire."}, {"id": "f043b649-6c31-4c76-9ab0-ae64b7665c28", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Ritual, art, and lived religion", "children": [{"id": "3cfab574-5a6b-468c-a4e8-a8d49ef7b5d8", "type": "concept", "title": "Temples, shrines, mosques, and monasteries"}, {"id": "756e77a4-d211-4a3f-be64-4cc8adf29b1c", "type": "concept", "title": "Iconography and visual culture"}, {"id": "b60ed94e-90f4-4209-9896-8bda2e2221d5", "type": "concept", "title": "Popular religion and local spirits"}], "description": "This subtopic keeps attention on practice, material culture, and everyday religious life."}]}, {"id": "47e8d177-b8fd-4ebb-be1e-030c7a98abad", "type": "topic", "title": "Imperial Formations and Inner Asian Connections", "description": "This section makes Inner Asia central rather than peripheral. Students study empires as mobile, negotiated systems that connected agrarian states, pastoral worlds, commercial routes, and military technologies.", "children": [{"id": "f1efa04a-b27a-4fff-8b7d-3ad07dd068d0", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Steppe politics and mobility", "children": [{"id": "edff0345-e866-453c-a81c-4cac8fbe703f", "type": "concept", "title": "Xiongnu and Turkic confederations"}, {"id": "b114aac0-623c-4056-9e33-943fc1dfd75a", "type": "concept", "title": "Pastoral economies"}, {"id": "b531f824-af55-476c-8b63-d0ba1b83945f", "type": "concept", "title": "Tribute and frontier diplomacy"}], "description": "Students learn why pastoral power, cavalry warfare, and alliance networks mattered to Asian state formation."}, {"id": "70eb8c9b-1ea3-4918-a49e-c19efb23c801", "type": "subtopic", "title": "The Mongol Empire", "children": [{"id": "dc899018-7551-46f9-9989-5241f230bfbc", "type": "concept", "title": "Chinggisid statecraft"}, {"id": "c76af055-1540-4c0e-a7a5-0256ba828fca", "type": "concept", "title": "Pax Mongolica and trade"}, {"id": "157daa92-0f3f-4d63-b7a3-6f42ccf8e647", "type": "concept", "title": "Transmission of technologies and disease"}], "description": "The point is to understand the Mongols as both conquerors and builders of a Eurasian imperial order."}, {"id": "eff36ee7-b242-4a17-94df-ebbcd67a316b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Frontiers and borderlands", "children": [{"id": "76e7eb11-db35-4ebe-b52b-9ef9ff6aa4f0", "type": "concept", "title": "Great Wall frontiers"}, {"id": "27fc6c43-9686-402d-83e4-d6f927d12b31", "type": "concept", "title": "Tibet, Xinjiang, and Central Asia"}, {"id": "833b53d9-9e8b-4ade-b399-2dda546fb710", "type": "concept", "title": "Korea, Vietnam, and tributary relations"}], "description": "Students analyze borders as zones of exchange, violence, and identity formation."}, {"id": "c067e781-072f-41d9-8fed-526f28354954", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Imperial knowledge and administration", "children": [{"id": "ea893836-2254-4197-b481-a9f9066ecd29", "type": "concept", "title": "Census and taxation"}, {"id": "2e803354-f8c2-44ef-a8f2-0d157c361908", "type": "concept", "title": "Maps and ethnography"}, {"id": "43c25385-6e79-4947-b785-502e57b966fc", "type": "concept", "title": "Multilingual governance"}], "description": "This shows how empires classified people, land, labor, and culture."}]}, {"id": "872f5778-59a4-40eb-8d93-dc072312bafb", "type": "topic", "title": "Medieval China, Korea, Japan, and Regional Systems", "description": "Students learn East Asia as a set of connected but distinct societies. The section balances China-centered regional systems with Korea, Japan, Vietnam, and maritime networks as active historical agents.", "children": [{"id": "47bc3247-25fd-4e0b-aa36-5ad31286e458", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Tang, Song, and Yuan transformations", "children": [{"id": "d348e39d-66c6-4cfe-9d98-08d68652d1d7", "type": "concept", "title": "Civil service and literati culture"}, {"id": "e1ede8f5-d251-4e83-96c2-dc5f66b4892a", "type": "concept", "title": "Urbanization and markets"}, {"id": "37a322e4-f17e-4ac2-9f38-482262cc5773", "type": "concept", "title": "Mongol rule in China"}], "description": "The gist is to connect state capacity, commercialization, technology, and cultural production."}, {"id": "e7615b2c-60eb-4ec4-8674-617c3a6b92bc", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Korean historical trajectories", "children": [{"id": "b56f47ac-babf-49f6-85ed-dc3517572bfd", "type": "concept", "title": "Silla and Koryo"}, {"id": "66e41d17-031e-4df1-ac22-5c6b32830e6d", "type": "concept", "title": "Choson statecraft"}, {"id": "d2a03e86-54bb-4331-9268-be4079996514", "type": "concept", "title": "Neo-Confucian social order"}], "description": "Students examine Korea through state formation, regional diplomacy, and social institutions."}, {"id": "fdcb5237-4bcf-4a5a-be8d-72ae85119446", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Japan before Tokugawa", "children": [{"id": "1b10df08-6a47-4fa7-af98-040d18586462", "type": "concept", "title": "Heian aristocracy"}, {"id": "13d96c29-ede6-4a0a-ab26-f43c3a50c4fd", "type": "concept", "title": "Kamakura and Muromachi orders"}, {"id": "60b2f090-88b0-46cb-9472-3bcb63237b03", "type": "concept", "title": "Sengoku conflict"}], "description": "This subtopic introduces court culture, warrior rule, religious institutions, and regional power."}, {"id": "2e8b1a85-4ec6-40d7-a7c0-8cd4f6791490", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Regional systems and maritime exchange", "children": [{"id": "7ea9c054-fa32-4cd6-b984-d5b589b03f76", "type": "concept", "title": "Tributary relations"}, {"id": "aaf56584-d391-49a5-ab47-f89549ee1cd7", "type": "concept", "title": "Japanese and Ryukyuan connections"}, {"id": "03be396f-e21f-4b53-8a8b-bfeacd712c3c", "type": "concept", "title": "Ports and merchants"}], "description": "Students see diplomacy, piracy, trade, migration, and culture as linked phenomena."}]}, {"id": "9a6cf828-c531-4965-8e15-da918e67fa68", "type": "topic", "title": "South Asia from Early Polities to the Mughal World", "description": "This section frames South Asia as a diverse and connected region with many centers of power. Students move beyond a single India narrative to examine caste, region, religion, empire, and oceanic exchange.", "children": [{"id": "9583be4e-51f7-45d2-9765-93ddcbecbf5d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Regional polities and social worlds", "children": [{"id": "3a5e9fec-5a67-499f-9f84-722fb5c85e2a", "type": "concept", "title": "Deccan and southern kingdoms"}, {"id": "14847182-7f09-49f2-9493-55f3f4030035", "type": "concept", "title": "Temple economies"}, {"id": "9f792220-332a-4713-9a43-36bc58c4abad", "type": "concept", "title": "Caste and social hierarchy"}], "description": "Students learn how political authority interacted with caste, kinship, landholding, and religious patronage."}, {"id": "4db06c15-40df-445c-a6e1-5c3e64c30964", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Delhi Sultanate and Indo-Islamic cultures", "children": [{"id": "abb85ef9-2c38-44de-ba58-57da3d6e8428", "type": "concept", "title": "Persianate political culture"}, {"id": "11042c79-a635-494a-85dd-79a7f77a7c71", "type": "concept", "title": "Urbanization"}, {"id": "8dbb1bfe-eb14-407c-a19d-307f4e839ec5", "type": "concept", "title": "Religious plurality"}], "description": "The point is to understand conquest, adaptation, and new cultural formations without reducing them to conflict."}, {"id": "86dbb835-5d42-4f08-8093-9581e5214329", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Mughal Empire", "children": [{"id": "439f517c-d0c1-4f72-bfa2-b64ac39c534b", "type": "concept", "title": "Akbar and imperial ideology"}, {"id": "bd5df7d5-2060-4e2a-9c72-7d8fb40bce2e", "type": "concept", "title": "Mansabdari and revenue systems"}, {"id": "06b90108-06ca-47b7-b4a2-02b33d2af88f", "type": "concept", "title": "Art, architecture, and court culture"}], "description": "Students examine Mughal rule as a sophisticated imperial system with regional variation and contested authority."}, {"id": "df09c5a1-a0cf-453a-aa41-6ef89a5b18d1", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Indian Ocean South Asia", "children": [{"id": "e5be60df-0556-4566-be7c-bed569d395d6", "type": "concept", "title": "Port cities"}, {"id": "9446697c-d183-4c3f-a1f4-80b63c4d14af", "type": "concept", "title": "Merchants and credit"}, {"id": "c4053802-45cc-48b1-8486-955a08b8f378", "type": "concept", "title": "Connections to Arabia, East Africa, and Southeast Asia"}], "description": "This broadens the region through trade, migration, pilgrimage, and diaspora."}]}, {"id": "8a6391d8-70f1-4959-9e97-1c942c42a143", "type": "topic", "title": "Southeast Asia, Indian Ocean Worlds, and Maritime Exchange", "description": "Southeast Asia is treated as a historically creative crossroads rather than a passive meeting point. Students study how local societies selected, reshaped, and resisted outside influences.", "children": [{"id": "b7f4418b-0479-4992-98cd-75694e18342d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Mandala states and political culture", "children": [{"id": "8e6743e9-8569-4074-886a-bfd0a3572fd8", "type": "concept", "title": "Angkor"}, {"id": "1f12ad29-6ed8-42e2-b441-a20d8da671f9", "type": "concept", "title": "Srivijaya and Majapahit"}, {"id": "126776cb-f1a3-4b8c-8f06-0eed10ec3e53", "type": "concept", "title": "Mainland and island polities"}], "description": "Students learn models of power based on centers, networks, ritual authority, and shifting allegiance."}, {"id": "6c289b9b-8947-4d6f-b697-8df510f22a5c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Islamization and religious plurality", "children": [{"id": "2617dcb0-fc70-4079-945a-1f8c06487347", "type": "concept", "title": "Malay-Indonesian Islam"}, {"id": "394e1afd-23f1-4444-9ebc-57b7b9db6b05", "type": "concept", "title": "Buddhist kingdoms"}, {"id": "957924fe-a932-4e6d-96f2-14742dad830b", "type": "concept", "title": "Christian missions"}], "description": "The gist is to trace religious change through trade, scholarship, court patronage, and local practice."}, {"id": "529a5abc-b672-464c-ac37-f5c0f01fcbba", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Maritime commerce", "children": [{"id": "4bad3265-88b5-4d0a-a6fc-ecc8616f09c7", "type": "concept", "title": "Straits of Malacca"}, {"id": "f811f573-2f56-4525-a43e-1133037518b5", "type": "concept", "title": "Chinese and Indian merchants"}, {"id": "d0842725-a3bf-4b01-96d1-5d8c17143c63", "type": "concept", "title": "European trading companies"}], "description": "Students connect spices, textiles, silver, ceramics, and labor to state and social change."}, {"id": "2b97a52e-7f28-4d76-9939-8079cafe1f57", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Colonial Southeast Asia", "children": [{"id": "d1fdb30a-4e04-4090-b4e5-db55542bb5a8", "type": "concept", "title": "Plantation economies"}, {"id": "714ae01f-b0e4-4c35-a3c3-4cc5bac0697a", "type": "concept", "title": "Missionaries and education"}, {"id": "69d8b272-edd0-4e30-a598-aa3d14aa5217", "type": "concept", "title": "Anti-colonial movements"}], "description": "This subtopic prepares students to compare Dutch, British, French, Spanish, and American imperial projects."}]}, {"id": "e2a80827-9d48-4868-ad03-a7153d315eea", "type": "topic", "title": "Early Modern Asia: Ming-Qing, Tokugawa, Joseon, Safavid-Mughal-Ottoman Connections", "description": "The course uses early modernity as a comparative problem, not a European template. Students ask how Asian states managed population, economy, knowledge, military power, and cross-cultural contact.", "children": [{"id": "1fefebc0-3766-442d-b4d6-ef710222835c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Ming and Qing China", "children": [{"id": "edbd18b8-219d-4123-ae8d-413571ba5ea3", "type": "concept", "title": "Ming maritime policy and commerce"}, {"id": "24cb7bf2-8dec-48d6-9e7e-08d862e62c77", "type": "concept", "title": "Qing conquest and multiethnic empire"}, {"id": "d780ff03-2d95-49b2-bb5f-06c08ea99a25", "type": "concept", "title": "Population and silver"}], "description": "Students study dynastic rule, commercialization, frontier expansion, and social change."}, {"id": "20aee15d-6a56-4fa9-81ca-a58f0701609d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Tokugawa Japan", "children": [{"id": "7ddfffb8-ea48-4be0-b1ff-67d5c34d4ae6", "type": "concept", "title": "Bakuhan system"}, {"id": "d362d144-3471-459a-913c-c8e90b660784", "type": "concept", "title": "Samurai, peasants, artisans, merchants"}, {"id": "42f40278-4ea3-4724-b970-44b2fde87f26", "type": "concept", "title": "Rangaku and global knowledge"}], "description": "The point is to understand peace, hierarchy, urban culture, and controlled foreign relations."}, {"id": "58684b45-18a1-4710-9ed8-f8345f69986f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Joseon Korea", "children": [{"id": "f8ceb1c1-40f5-4890-b2b2-d5b59cb53b5b", "type": "concept", "title": "Yangban elite"}, {"id": "5fc9a556-2f50-480d-a8cf-c25570e60cd5", "type": "concept", "title": "Ritual and family order"}, {"id": "fe202a1b-da58-42a1-91d1-738d29ed8de0", "type": "concept", "title": "Tributary diplomacy"}], "description": "Students analyze Neo-Confucian governance, social hierarchy, diplomacy, and reform debates."}, {"id": "84670a54-d93a-43a7-a25c-81f240331a39", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Connected Islamic empires", "children": [{"id": "d9b94072-7dbe-4e43-b905-1109450dcd2c", "type": "concept", "title": "Gunpowder empires"}, {"id": "256915d1-0b61-4dd0-90e2-86cbd1755a51", "type": "concept", "title": "Law and religious authority"}, {"id": "b6b8ce56-5caa-4714-898c-a18a4256f157", "type": "concept", "title": "Artistic and commercial circulation"}], "description": "This compares Ottoman, Safavid, and Mughal worlds as linked early modern formations."}]}, {"id": "daefc8e9-b753-450d-ab4b-92f1129b2015", "type": "topic", "title": "Colonialism, Imperialism, and Unequal Treaties", "description": "This section focuses on coercion, collaboration, reform, resistance, and knowledge production. Students learn that imperialism was military and economic, but also legal, cultural, racial, and archival.", "children": [{"id": "e1baee42-9fba-4d11-8654-c2a06ea0eb65", "type": "subtopic", "title": "European imperial systems", "children": [{"id": "48a5ef3f-2903-4eff-bf29-bd92e221f388", "type": "concept", "title": "British India"}, {"id": "63d017e2-99aa-4570-b669-dfd3a1ba73df", "type": "concept", "title": "Dutch East Indies"}, {"id": "611267db-00db-4ae9-906b-7b3e3cd3c7ea", "type": "concept", "title": "French Indochina"}], "description": "Students compare direct rule, indirect rule, company rule, and settler or plantation economies."}, {"id": "30dc4532-c5f2-46d3-ab9f-396938b12602", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Unequal treaties and treaty ports", "children": [{"id": "5a82f7ac-927c-42c5-bfe6-503b84e1bd3d", "type": "concept", "title": "Opium Wars"}, {"id": "54fa89a6-fde1-42b8-bba6-dd5152b9478f", "type": "concept", "title": "Treaty-port society"}, {"id": "2747a29c-49f4-42c8-8291-f0a286375fbc", "type": "concept", "title": "Missionaries and reformers"}], "description": "The gist is to understand sovereignty under pressure through law, diplomacy, commerce, and extraterritoriality."}, {"id": "25ac475f-fdd9-4933-ad26-d71cdf41ec99", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Japanese imperialism", "children": [{"id": "cdad64c7-19a7-4bdb-a320-efc7aee3077f", "type": "concept", "title": "Meiji state-building"}, {"id": "b719295d-2e58-4a81-9d5b-4144b38cea1f", "type": "concept", "title": "Korea and Taiwan"}, {"id": "d0c67bf0-2444-4ea2-8e60-23a5385aab9d", "type": "concept", "title": "Manchuria and total war"}], "description": "Students examine Japan as both a target of imperial pressure and an imperial power."}, {"id": "c761e499-8991-4523-8ae4-ba1f0a8d7e67", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Colonial knowledge and race", "children": [{"id": "d4f154f1-be20-41ba-a794-58b3a49518f2", "type": "concept", "title": "Orientalism"}, {"id": "42c60cc1-77c6-4067-b315-b74a4f9b6d2b", "type": "concept", "title": "Caste and ethnicity under empire"}, {"id": "ae127f6c-263e-41cc-8ed2-a3fe2caa121d", "type": "concept", "title": "Colonial museums and archives"}], "description": "This subtopic shows how censuses, ethnography, law, and education created categories that shaped rule."}]}, {"id": "60170e15-0dbf-4875-a714-824b2cf5da11", "type": "topic", "title": "Reform, Revolution, Nationalism, and State-Building", "description": "Students examine how Asian intellectuals, activists, workers, peasants, soldiers, and elites answered the crisis of empire. The goal is to compare political imaginaries without assuming a single path to modernity.", "children": [{"id": "395c78d6-58a7-42bd-ba2e-a9e8662ead42", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Reform movements", "children": [{"id": "a2e21df4-e7b5-45b3-b54a-9878533e71c3", "type": "concept", "title": "Late Qing reforms"}, {"id": "4103224c-15c4-48d9-9b9f-9da4dd3a2ef1", "type": "concept", "title": "Meiji reforms"}, {"id": "d439e365-e446-4a3c-b43d-f3ac2de520c1", "type": "concept", "title": "Ottoman and South Asian reform currents"}], "description": "Students see reform as a contested mix of institutional change, moral renewal, and survival strategy."}, {"id": "e18ec079-c379-4173-8868-058d3c83cb71", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Nationalism and anti-colonial politics", "children": [{"id": "a02be1ca-f575-4394-a84a-268193f3f078", "type": "concept", "title": "Indian National Congress"}, {"id": "78b48606-e65a-4f10-8505-0666c77ef20e", "type": "concept", "title": "Chinese nationalism"}, {"id": "2f89a704-6867-4697-95ba-f2570a7729f2", "type": "concept", "title": "Vietnamese, Indonesian, and Korean movements"}], "description": "The point is to connect mass politics with print, education, language, religion, and organization."}, {"id": "cc0a82dd-4c4d-423b-9fb5-f3442334d1b9", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Revolutionary ideologies", "children": [{"id": "43088d13-9e1a-4aeb-949b-1873ab5b9fb2", "type": "concept", "title": "1911 Revolution"}, {"id": "c19def8b-b3f3-43c9-b7ad-2571b3634c91", "type": "concept", "title": "May Fourth"}, {"id": "735f6e74-ba9a-45d0-8d99-44cd92c09158", "type": "concept", "title": "Peasant and labor mobilization"}], "description": "Students compare liberal, socialist, communist, religious, and monarchist visions of political order."}, {"id": "2c8b90dc-734f-4869-9b95-5028f0952f00", "type": "subtopic", "title": "State-building and citizenship", "children": [{"id": "d9a88a60-bdcb-4f5f-89a1-1fa32d341996", "type": "concept", "title": "Constitutions"}, {"id": "d97b20ed-8678-4443-9e04-c67eb5d4622a", "type": "concept", "title": "Minorities and borders"}, {"id": "9d085de9-ec4a-451f-8c68-4d8796654ea8", "type": "concept", "title": "Education and national culture"}], "description": "This subtopic asks who belonged in new states and on what terms."}]}, {"id": "9518ae8e-906d-4a9e-8545-08605a5b8b8f", "type": "topic", "title": "War, Occupation, Partition, and Memory in the Twentieth Century", "description": "This section confronts traumatic histories with care and analytical rigor. Students learn how violence reshapes states and societies, and how memory politics continues long after formal conflict ends.", "children": [{"id": "ccd7b3e1-65b5-4511-9537-766bb9ada8a8", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Asia-Pacific War", "children": [{"id": "8598627c-7b8a-4d65-8383-53d3caf00d5d", "type": "concept", "title": "Japanese occupation"}, {"id": "8ad445ab-4146-4464-a592-13dcc10d7f79", "type": "concept", "title": "Nanjing and atrocity debates"}, {"id": "50201592-51ad-4031-a303-3dd601df70e3", "type": "concept", "title": "Comfort women and forced labor"}], "description": "Students study war as regional catastrophe, imperial project, occupation regime, and memory problem."}, {"id": "472dc506-8505-4dde-b750-d3b886ad336e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Civil war and revolution", "children": [{"id": "019db48f-e35d-4088-8c25-ce2ff2b5a269", "type": "concept", "title": "Chinese Civil War"}, {"id": "1e0265bf-9144-46e8-9c1b-0c9e56676eac", "type": "concept", "title": "Korean War"}, {"id": "5c92e2a4-f755-4467-a6fc-c22023186ebf", "type": "concept", "title": "Vietnamese revolutionary wars"}], "description": "The gist is to connect military conflict with social revolution and state formation."}, {"id": "ea869490-a8cf-412d-b337-b17d2a41cb5b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Partition and displacement", "children": [{"id": "9a95820f-e358-483f-ad91-9005b1740b8b", "type": "concept", "title": "India and Pakistan"}, {"id": "f189dd14-8468-4b03-9be6-0c2c082e8210", "type": "concept", "title": "Korea"}, {"id": "1f434729-b3e6-45ac-a4ef-cf54878eef69", "type": "concept", "title": "Palestine and wider Asian contexts"}], "description": "Students analyze partition as border-making, mass migration, violence, and long-term political inheritance."}, {"id": "8c18f889-0d9c-4438-b1ed-e701372017ee", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Memory and public history", "children": [{"id": "53447e42-ea36-4827-92ac-b498ae0aa927", "type": "concept", "title": "Museums and memorials"}, {"id": "b7dd92f9-df0f-499f-9a60-576ea5a6bd7a", "type": "concept", "title": "Textbook controversies"}, {"id": "cb6df8d7-d729-46dd-8991-d94dab04f2bc", "type": "concept", "title": "Transitional justice"}], "description": "This subtopic asks how societies remember, deny, commemorate, and litigate the past."}]}, {"id": "8d18ea3a-db80-4af3-9e49-9e3b8257d53f", "type": "topic", "title": "Communism, Cold War Asia, Developmental States, and Political Economy", "description": "The Cold War in Asia was not merely a U.S.-Soviet overlay. Students study local revolutions, regional wars, developmental ambitions, and global capitalism as intertwined historical processes.", "children": [{"id": "d8063ff8-a0b6-41a3-ba79-4927c0588757", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Socialist revolutions and regimes", "children": [{"id": "f0bb14ea-144c-4606-bf89-ab388cc7fb27", "type": "concept", "title": "People's Republic of China"}, {"id": "7115448b-dfb7-4ad1-9e97-9c00b931fcd4", "type": "concept", "title": "Vietnam"}, {"id": "f44852ac-ffb0-4acd-9880-2034c756d998", "type": "concept", "title": "Cambodia and Khmer Rouge"}], "description": "Students compare revolutionary legitimacy, land reform, party-state building, and social engineering."}, {"id": "d9dfa616-801a-4238-b2c8-60259dd4aa14", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Developmental states", "children": [{"id": "d5b2664a-e349-4dbe-9ade-082d19de366d", "type": "concept", "title": "Japan's postwar growth"}, {"id": "a14bb157-8f9c-43a7-9fca-ebcd4d737125", "type": "concept", "title": "South Korea and Taiwan"}, {"id": "904cff54-3740-4603-9801-72fa380bad7c", "type": "concept", "title": "Singapore and Southeast Asian models"}], "description": "The point is to understand rapid industrialization through state policy, labor discipline, capital, and geopolitics."}, {"id": "62d66785-674c-46c0-9d99-eebf58da00de", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Cold War security orders", "children": [{"id": "d6cd6889-3681-482b-a8e0-67847a48c4e1", "type": "concept", "title": "U.S. alliances"}, {"id": "ce250b93-b7ae-4fd3-a2f1-691e645976e9", "type": "concept", "title": "Nonalignment"}, {"id": "c10a4be1-322c-4987-ae8d-ac670ad563a7", "type": "concept", "title": "Insurgency and counterinsurgency"}], "description": "Students learn how alliances, intervention, and military regimes shaped domestic politics."}, {"id": "e4505074-e0b0-4855-8fd9-915c29e9ad3d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Reform, markets, and inequality", "children": [{"id": "6afe5133-12f4-44e7-b239-a2efd0963411", "type": "concept", "title": "China after 1978"}, {"id": "c0ee5cf6-6ec9-4349-b2ac-07ae7ae75c3f", "type": "concept", "title": "India after 1991"}, {"id": "dbc917bc-0387-4f3c-8a09-5d0c7bab8cb6", "type": "concept", "title": "Export economies and labor regimes"}], "description": "This subtopic links liberalization to migration, urbanization, environment, and social stratification."}]}, {"id": "bd33f90d-a027-46dd-b34f-513120d9458c", "type": "topic", "title": "Society, Gender, Labor, Migration, and Everyday Life", "description": "This section shifts from state narratives to lived history. Students learn how ordinary people made, endured, negotiated, and transformed large historical structures.", "children": [{"id": "05462723-fec8-415a-b4f6-4606c1647ec5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Gender and family", "children": [{"id": "e87c67e2-25c6-4b9c-aa8e-eb5ea593286f", "type": "concept", "title": "Patriarchy and household authority"}, {"id": "df46db46-dc5e-4956-9eba-0d6f80a45e6b", "type": "concept", "title": "Women in reform and revolution"}, {"id": "c54480bf-610f-41e4-93ae-721fc47f36dd", "type": "concept", "title": "Gender and empire"}], "description": "Students analyze law, labor, kinship, sexuality, reform, and feminist movements across periods and regions."}, {"id": "1432ceba-34ce-4dd1-8018-c91ee9c5ee5b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Labor and class", "children": [{"id": "392a27d7-dfe6-455e-8118-4626681c42bc", "type": "concept", "title": "Peasant labor"}, {"id": "62b1d92e-e10f-4754-a4b0-ecf42ef9ef38", "type": "concept", "title": "Industrial workers"}, {"id": "8d5fedce-45c9-4acb-9be3-9b91b0d56d6d", "type": "concept", "title": "Migrant and informal labor"}], "description": "The gist is to connect work to capitalism, empire, socialism, and development."}, {"id": "f719c930-74af-48bd-95d3-f0aa179382ba", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Migration and diaspora", "children": [{"id": "355527f0-ec62-4acd-bc9e-a2854c850cb7", "type": "concept", "title": "Chinese diaspora"}, {"id": "0f7650da-2bf4-4296-aed7-c423a9d43aa1", "type": "concept", "title": "South Asian diaspora"}, {"id": "0f758318-6cff-4c14-97bf-5420c6d6adf8", "type": "concept", "title": "Refugees and transnational families"}], "description": "Students trace Asian mobility across oceans, empires, borders, and labor systems."}, {"id": "e6f79e82-10ef-4527-9c5a-094421b631b9", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Urban and popular culture", "children": [{"id": "988885e2-83f4-41b0-ae24-688ccdefc3bd", "type": "concept", "title": "Cities and housing"}, {"id": "279ab484-95ab-4bf7-919e-54719c264b7f", "type": "concept", "title": "Film, music, and print culture"}, {"id": "6e24e97e-ed96-4d11-b519-48720bd6989c", "type": "concept", "title": "Consumption and youth cultures"}], "description": "This subtopic uses everyday life to understand modernity, identity, and social aspiration."}]}, {"id": "7d1e1de1-fa59-4212-8eee-2002f2b629b7", "type": "topic", "title": "Contemporary Asia: Globalization, Environment, Technology, and Geopolitics", "description": "The final substantive unit treats the present as history in motion. Students use historical thinking to analyze contemporary disputes, economic systems, environmental crises, and cultural change.", "children": [{"id": "0f628d42-8201-436a-be71-f6f94ce390d7", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Globalization and regional integration", "children": [{"id": "6ca11425-3263-4b5d-a8e0-df333269d6ed", "type": "concept", "title": "ASEAN and regional institutions"}, {"id": "45c85195-b777-48aa-90f1-b8595fb67172", "type": "concept", "title": "Belt and Road"}, {"id": "90487493-3c2a-451a-98cc-b467887c918f", "type": "concept", "title": "Global supply chains"}], "description": "Students examine production chains, trade, finance, tourism, and cultural exchange."}, {"id": "29c52ca4-1838-4824-a3cc-7bad3d152d91", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Environment and climate", "children": [{"id": "ed384838-5506-4c11-a8b7-f1dc24b42bf0", "type": "concept", "title": "Dams and rivers"}, {"id": "01b9949a-fce4-41e8-b3c2-a5d841d1a6c6", "type": "concept", "title": "Pollution and public health"}, {"id": "5e4443f1-c18e-43d6-9970-6aaffe5c6e9a", "type": "concept", "title": "Climate vulnerability and adaptation"}], "description": "The point is to study environmental change as political, economic, and social history."}, {"id": "d6adebea-e20b-40b7-a021-1f3f3b62506a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Technology and media", "children": [{"id": "e25d0a4b-dd15-47aa-87e2-afff3421981a", "type": "concept", "title": "Internet governance"}, {"id": "8e4a54be-8bc9-46e9-ac69-5c08bb819fa9", "type": "concept", "title": "K-pop, anime, Bollywood, and soft power"}, {"id": "d955415a-8e82-4e45-a3ac-c6106820e58c", "type": "concept", "title": "AI and labor futures"}], "description": "Students connect digital infrastructure, surveillance, platforms, and popular culture to historical change."}, {"id": "c3169e35-8c70-4ecc-84af-10fea413760f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Geopolitics and historical memory", "children": [{"id": "3989d251-d1de-4bc5-9050-515ff6d882b4", "type": "concept", "title": "Taiwan Strait"}, {"id": "9bf789b6-16c4-4ff2-b535-eb32f4f13e2b", "type": "concept", "title": "South China Sea"}, {"id": "f3faf819-a66f-45de-93eb-7a8b89c7b3bd", "type": "concept", "title": "Kashmir, Korea, and border disputes"}], "description": "This subtopic asks how history shapes current conflicts and diplomacy."}]}, {"id": "f7fb249b-0fab-443a-8078-1f63ebb8e1b6", "type": "topic", "title": "Historical Research Lab: Archives, Digital Methods, and Comparative Argument", "description": "This capstone section turns course knowledge into historical practice. Students learn to ask a researchable question, locate evidence, situate themselves in scholarship, and make a careful argument.", "children": [{"id": "d19ad842-d02e-42cb-9efc-374d4be3cf07", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Research question and scope", "children": [{"id": "822b8ffe-9513-4302-a63e-15ca05e942e6", "type": "concept", "title": "Topic narrowing"}, {"id": "ba0e9671-8cac-491d-86be-4ee9b2e9cdc0", "type": "concept", "title": "Periodization"}, {"id": "1d9f4705-7c61-471b-9f60-6f04b9e7bdbf", "type": "concept", "title": "Regional and comparative scope"}], "description": "Students learn to turn broad interest into a focused, answerable historical problem."}, {"id": "43944560-30e8-4eac-80a0-e46555d563a2", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Primary-source strategy", "children": [{"id": "0dc89a89-6183-4a4b-859e-8e602cebf862", "type": "concept", "title": "Archives and digitized collections"}, {"id": "0dadd00d-1dba-4c81-b9c7-52ae50b156b8", "type": "concept", "title": "Translation limits"}, {"id": "f7e090c3-f80f-4f40-a2b5-e7b1b854eb3c", "type": "concept", "title": "Source provenance and bias"}], "description": "The gist is to build an evidence base that is feasible, ethical, and analytically meaningful."}, {"id": "e9cdc351-1168-4db1-b315-ee545915bb39", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Historiographic intervention", "children": [{"id": "8b941437-6831-4929-93c0-dd19d157bad7", "type": "concept", "title": "Annotated bibliography"}, {"id": "f43e9e32-624b-41e2-a4eb-aacea72723f6", "type": "concept", "title": "Schools of interpretation"}, {"id": "cea92bbf-38c5-4464-87fd-4c530b07ca3a", "type": "concept", "title": "Argument positioning"}], "description": "Students learn to identify what scholars have argued and where their project contributes."}, {"id": "190e52e8-5cb6-4836-a5e4-5a80a3b86b76", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Digital and public history options", "children": [{"id": "33acb058-d288-4a23-8753-1655fdab386c", "type": "concept", "title": "GIS and spatial history"}, {"id": "4988c79d-8230-497f-940a-51551661acd4", "type": "concept", "title": "Text mining and metadata"}, {"id": "08d3300b-8a1c-460f-9a48-2ea6afc37e5e", "type": "concept", "title": "Public-facing exhibit design"}], "description": "This subtopic lets students use maps, timelines, datasets, exhibits, or visual analysis when appropriate."}]}], "dbBacked": true}$asiajson$::jsonb,
  true,
  false,
  'course',
  'public'
where not exists (
  select 1 from public.learning_paths where slug = 'asian-history'
);

update public.learning_paths
set
  title = 'Asian History',
  goal = 'Asian History',
  summary = 'Asian History is an advanced undergraduate course that introduces students to the major political, social, cultural, religious, economic, imperial, and environmental histories of Asia from ancient foundations to the present. The course treats Asia as a connected but internally diverse field, integrating East Asia, South Asia, Southeast Asia, Central and Inner Asia, and Islamic and Indian Ocean worlds. Students work with primary sources, material culture, maps, visual evidence, historiography, and digital archives while building a final source-based historical argument.',
  data = $asiajson${"slug": "asian-history", "title": "Asian History", "description": "Asian History is an advanced undergraduate course that introduces students to the major political, social, cultural, religious, economic, imperial, and environmental histories of Asia from ancient foundations to the present. The course treats Asia as a connected but internally diverse field, integrating East Asia, South Asia, Southeast Asia, Central and Inner Asia, and Islamic and Indian Ocean worlds. Students work with primary sources, material culture, maps, visual evidence, historiography, and digital archives while building a final source-based historical argument.", "topics": [{"id": "bf579dab-bcb7-4252-aca8-7d81250cb2b6", "type": "topic", "title": "Framing Asian History: Regions, Scales, Sources, and Historiography", "description": "This opening section gives students the intellectual map for the course. The point is to avoid treating Asia as a single civilization or a backdrop for Western expansion, and instead to ask how historians construct regions, periods, archives, and arguments.", "children": [{"id": "1f8ee1ec-7046-4e2f-ade3-6fadd56a2a0f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Asia as a historical category", "children": [{"id": "9e32dc31-db71-42d8-a36b-7917b6856782", "type": "concept", "title": "Area studies and its critiques"}, {"id": "effdbaaf-3d46-43a2-a0ad-1c8f43655918", "type": "concept", "title": "Continental, maritime, and borderland perspectives"}, {"id": "96d4e0d7-64a5-4a7e-b5e9-95650a32417b", "type": "concept", "title": "Avoiding civilizational essentialism"}], "description": "Students learn why East, South, Southeast, Central, and West Asia are useful but imperfect analytical labels."}, {"id": "e55863af-e1c9-4634-b076-d5a24f42efe1", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Historical scale and comparison", "children": [{"id": "78be1e9d-07dc-4f47-b7de-f63c5fb29eea", "type": "concept", "title": "Local case studies"}, {"id": "0073ab06-8d73-40f4-9eb3-0e81e2b867e9", "type": "concept", "title": "Regional comparison"}, {"id": "56a77066-e0f8-47d6-a031-e4d333b1f29a", "type": "concept", "title": "Transnational and connected histories"}], "description": "The gist is to move confidently between village, dynasty, empire, ocean, diaspora, and global system."}, {"id": "20a01d51-e29f-4175-afd3-9dd6cbb25f83", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Sources and archives", "children": [{"id": "989a7dec-143c-4833-80b9-c241ec4490a7", "type": "concept", "title": "Language and translation"}, {"id": "ed1934bb-d315-42a1-a8f8-32763ee72abe", "type": "concept", "title": "Material culture"}, {"id": "2ffddde3-3b8b-4548-9524-1efbdf0910d8", "type": "concept", "title": "Colonial archives and archival silences"}], "description": "Students see how texts, inscriptions, objects, images, maps, oral histories, and state records produce different kinds of evidence."}, {"id": "07f91800-832c-46df-b531-d5f64370cb83", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Historiographic debates", "children": [{"id": "057addfb-737d-4e48-9c92-cf9cc54d8cfe", "type": "concept", "title": "Orientalism and area studies"}, {"id": "870e7151-1e49-48ce-a0bb-65e389c6fb4e", "type": "concept", "title": "Nationalist historiography"}, {"id": "3ed07e68-1035-4d33-b2da-85083545f975", "type": "concept", "title": "Subaltern and postcolonial approaches"}], "description": "This introduces the big interpretive disagreements that will recur throughout the course."}]}, {"id": "861b90c7-f93b-47d1-849a-1c5227dbe9b2", "type": "topic", "title": "Ancient and Classical Foundations: South, East, Southeast, and Central Asia", "description": "This section establishes the deep structures that later states and societies inherited, contested, and reworked. Students focus on political formation, writing, social hierarchy, and exchange rather than memorizing dynasties in isolation.", "children": [{"id": "1c14e279-b4c5-4af2-823a-6865ec4ca46b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Early urbanism and states", "children": [{"id": "1fb24917-eb55-49c7-ace1-d15ba29167a2", "type": "concept", "title": "Indus Valley"}, {"id": "4dec8463-e20e-4788-8d3f-ff8cf0c70110", "type": "concept", "title": "Shang and Zhou China"}, {"id": "a53cfaff-3fc4-4b82-9109-cab1b50847f7", "type": "concept", "title": "Early Southeast Asian polities"}], "description": "The point is to compare how early complex societies organized power, labor, ritual, and space."}, {"id": "7b3f38b3-59c2-46c5-8de2-8629ccbc8bea", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Classical political cultures", "children": [{"id": "a6665415-2888-4411-92bf-2389e4875dc0", "type": "concept", "title": "Mauryan and Gupta worlds"}, {"id": "eb6a0ebe-4e56-411b-9309-71f0cf68dd52", "type": "concept", "title": "Qin-Han statecraft"}, {"id": "ac18b490-33b9-47ea-9195-f6772d112936", "type": "concept", "title": "Mandate, bureaucracy, and law"}], "description": "Students learn how rulers justified authority and how institutions shaped social order."}, {"id": "be9a9fe0-8a1f-495d-a2eb-bbdbf3112492", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Writing, memory, and recordkeeping", "children": [{"id": "d6994224-483f-4555-bfb9-d8ae09d61a4a", "type": "concept", "title": "Inscriptions"}, {"id": "13456b96-e399-4394-a28d-ebdf0f41d7a6", "type": "concept", "title": "Chronicles and court histories"}, {"id": "9bc7340b-a288-4d52-aa0d-d6bf7fafb0c3", "type": "concept", "title": "Archaeological evidence"}], "description": "This subtopic shows how historical knowledge depends on durable media and elite preservation."}, {"id": "8778c940-ef19-40b1-965f-c60c89f8516b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Exchange before empire", "children": [{"id": "5fd25202-1538-419c-9ed4-03b51aed7736", "type": "concept", "title": "Silk Roads"}, {"id": "271fb9eb-885a-4d92-87ab-5d62314a1c00", "type": "concept", "title": "Indian Ocean exchange"}, {"id": "ef2fe401-eeee-4101-956f-8cdb30b33669", "type": "concept", "title": "Steppe-sedentary interaction"}], "description": "Students trace early movement of goods, peoples, and ideas across land and sea."}]}, {"id": "4bb8ce50-99db-4578-9da8-338b69279723", "type": "topic", "title": "Religions, Ideas, and Cultural Worlds", "description": "Religion and thought are treated here as institutions, ethical systems, social practices, and transregional networks. Students learn to connect belief, patronage, law, art, pilgrimage, and political legitimacy.", "children": [{"id": "d2f85aae-026e-487f-bac5-dc472cb2ac48", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Buddhist circulation", "children": [{"id": "dfd0b57f-b027-43db-9bc0-556c419416bf", "type": "concept", "title": "South Asian origins"}, {"id": "a12236aa-1c9b-4b3d-84d9-3302488f89a4", "type": "concept", "title": "Central Asian transmission"}, {"id": "bf0b75fd-6eac-4c7f-a5ee-b9c0abdbcb4f", "type": "concept", "title": "China, Korea, Japan, and Southeast Asia"}], "description": "Students examine Buddhism as a mobile tradition shaped by translation, monastic networks, patronage, and local adaptation."}, {"id": "efeadd04-88dc-4e07-8f7d-2549865061c3", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Confucian, Daoist, and legal traditions", "children": [{"id": "117dd49c-c041-4ee4-aa90-e4218b0695aa", "type": "concept", "title": "Classics and education"}, {"id": "d907043b-c030-407b-a744-4660b4ed86c7", "type": "concept", "title": "Ritual and hierarchy"}, {"id": "34279496-8a36-4ef2-94a7-2101f8508e66", "type": "concept", "title": "State orthodoxy and dissent"}], "description": "The gist is to see East Asian governance and family order as historically produced and debated."}, {"id": "670e8006-1b6a-4060-8e1d-827b05d724bf", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Islamic worlds in Asia", "children": [{"id": "a9a91330-3f9a-49e4-8d99-a54f93c9df16", "type": "concept", "title": "Caliphates and ulama"}, {"id": "1a5e8d66-7672-4fdf-b612-eb5b91a48c20", "type": "concept", "title": "Sufi networks"}, {"id": "17a0a119-b432-4133-b77b-603f86b41f92", "type": "concept", "title": "Islam in South and Southeast Asia"}], "description": "Students learn how Islam linked West, Central, South, and Southeast Asia through trade, law, scholarship, and empire."}, {"id": "f043b649-6c31-4c76-9ab0-ae64b7665c28", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Ritual, art, and lived religion", "children": [{"id": "3cfab574-5a6b-468c-a4e8-a8d49ef7b5d8", "type": "concept", "title": "Temples, shrines, mosques, and monasteries"}, {"id": "756e77a4-d211-4a3f-be64-4cc8adf29b1c", "type": "concept", "title": "Iconography and visual culture"}, {"id": "b60ed94e-90f4-4209-9896-8bda2e2221d5", "type": "concept", "title": "Popular religion and local spirits"}], "description": "This subtopic keeps attention on practice, material culture, and everyday religious life."}]}, {"id": "47e8d177-b8fd-4ebb-be1e-030c7a98abad", "type": "topic", "title": "Imperial Formations and Inner Asian Connections", "description": "This section makes Inner Asia central rather than peripheral. Students study empires as mobile, negotiated systems that connected agrarian states, pastoral worlds, commercial routes, and military technologies.", "children": [{"id": "f1efa04a-b27a-4fff-8b7d-3ad07dd068d0", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Steppe politics and mobility", "children": [{"id": "edff0345-e866-453c-a81c-4cac8fbe703f", "type": "concept", "title": "Xiongnu and Turkic confederations"}, {"id": "b114aac0-623c-4056-9e33-943fc1dfd75a", "type": "concept", "title": "Pastoral economies"}, {"id": "b531f824-af55-476c-8b63-d0ba1b83945f", "type": "concept", "title": "Tribute and frontier diplomacy"}], "description": "Students learn why pastoral power, cavalry warfare, and alliance networks mattered to Asian state formation."}, {"id": "70eb8c9b-1ea3-4918-a49e-c19efb23c801", "type": "subtopic", "title": "The Mongol Empire", "children": [{"id": "dc899018-7551-46f9-9989-5241f230bfbc", "type": "concept", "title": "Chinggisid statecraft"}, {"id": "c76af055-1540-4c0e-a7a5-0256ba828fca", "type": "concept", "title": "Pax Mongolica and trade"}, {"id": "157daa92-0f3f-4d63-b7a3-6f42ccf8e647", "type": "concept", "title": "Transmission of technologies and disease"}], "description": "The point is to understand the Mongols as both conquerors and builders of a Eurasian imperial order."}, {"id": "eff36ee7-b242-4a17-94df-ebbcd67a316b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Frontiers and borderlands", "children": [{"id": "76e7eb11-db35-4ebe-b52b-9ef9ff6aa4f0", "type": "concept", "title": "Great Wall frontiers"}, {"id": "27fc6c43-9686-402d-83e4-d6f927d12b31", "type": "concept", "title": "Tibet, Xinjiang, and Central Asia"}, {"id": "833b53d9-9e8b-4ade-b399-2dda546fb710", "type": "concept", "title": "Korea, Vietnam, and tributary relations"}], "description": "Students analyze borders as zones of exchange, violence, and identity formation."}, {"id": "c067e781-072f-41d9-8fed-526f28354954", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Imperial knowledge and administration", "children": [{"id": "ea893836-2254-4197-b481-a9f9066ecd29", "type": "concept", "title": "Census and taxation"}, {"id": "2e803354-f8c2-44ef-a8f2-0d157c361908", "type": "concept", "title": "Maps and ethnography"}, {"id": "43c25385-6e79-4947-b785-502e57b966fc", "type": "concept", "title": "Multilingual governance"}], "description": "This shows how empires classified people, land, labor, and culture."}]}, {"id": "872f5778-59a4-40eb-8d93-dc072312bafb", "type": "topic", "title": "Medieval China, Korea, Japan, and Regional Systems", "description": "Students learn East Asia as a set of connected but distinct societies. The section balances China-centered regional systems with Korea, Japan, Vietnam, and maritime networks as active historical agents.", "children": [{"id": "47bc3247-25fd-4e0b-aa36-5ad31286e458", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Tang, Song, and Yuan transformations", "children": [{"id": "d348e39d-66c6-4cfe-9d98-08d68652d1d7", "type": "concept", "title": "Civil service and literati culture"}, {"id": "e1ede8f5-d251-4e83-96c2-dc5f66b4892a", "type": "concept", "title": "Urbanization and markets"}, {"id": "37a322e4-f17e-4ac2-9f38-482262cc5773", "type": "concept", "title": "Mongol rule in China"}], "description": "The gist is to connect state capacity, commercialization, technology, and cultural production."}, {"id": "e7615b2c-60eb-4ec4-8674-617c3a6b92bc", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Korean historical trajectories", "children": [{"id": "b56f47ac-babf-49f6-85ed-dc3517572bfd", "type": "concept", "title": "Silla and Koryo"}, {"id": "66e41d17-031e-4df1-ac22-5c6b32830e6d", "type": "concept", "title": "Choson statecraft"}, {"id": "d2a03e86-54bb-4331-9268-be4079996514", "type": "concept", "title": "Neo-Confucian social order"}], "description": "Students examine Korea through state formation, regional diplomacy, and social institutions."}, {"id": "fdcb5237-4bcf-4a5a-be8d-72ae85119446", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Japan before Tokugawa", "children": [{"id": "1b10df08-6a47-4fa7-af98-040d18586462", "type": "concept", "title": "Heian aristocracy"}, {"id": "13d96c29-ede6-4a0a-ab26-f43c3a50c4fd", "type": "concept", "title": "Kamakura and Muromachi orders"}, {"id": "60b2f090-88b0-46cb-9472-3bcb63237b03", "type": "concept", "title": "Sengoku conflict"}], "description": "This subtopic introduces court culture, warrior rule, religious institutions, and regional power."}, {"id": "2e8b1a85-4ec6-40d7-a7c0-8cd4f6791490", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Regional systems and maritime exchange", "children": [{"id": "7ea9c054-fa32-4cd6-b984-d5b589b03f76", "type": "concept", "title": "Tributary relations"}, {"id": "aaf56584-d391-49a5-ab47-f89549ee1cd7", "type": "concept", "title": "Japanese and Ryukyuan connections"}, {"id": "03be396f-e21f-4b53-8a8b-bfeacd712c3c", "type": "concept", "title": "Ports and merchants"}], "description": "Students see diplomacy, piracy, trade, migration, and culture as linked phenomena."}]}, {"id": "9a6cf828-c531-4965-8e15-da918e67fa68", "type": "topic", "title": "South Asia from Early Polities to the Mughal World", "description": "This section frames South Asia as a diverse and connected region with many centers of power. Students move beyond a single India narrative to examine caste, region, religion, empire, and oceanic exchange.", "children": [{"id": "9583be4e-51f7-45d2-9765-93ddcbecbf5d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Regional polities and social worlds", "children": [{"id": "3a5e9fec-5a67-499f-9f84-722fb5c85e2a", "type": "concept", "title": "Deccan and southern kingdoms"}, {"id": "14847182-7f09-49f2-9493-55f3f4030035", "type": "concept", "title": "Temple economies"}, {"id": "9f792220-332a-4713-9a43-36bc58c4abad", "type": "concept", "title": "Caste and social hierarchy"}], "description": "Students learn how political authority interacted with caste, kinship, landholding, and religious patronage."}, {"id": "4db06c15-40df-445c-a6e1-5c3e64c30964", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Delhi Sultanate and Indo-Islamic cultures", "children": [{"id": "abb85ef9-2c38-44de-ba58-57da3d6e8428", "type": "concept", "title": "Persianate political culture"}, {"id": "11042c79-a635-494a-85dd-79a7f77a7c71", "type": "concept", "title": "Urbanization"}, {"id": "8dbb1bfe-eb14-407c-a19d-307f4e839ec5", "type": "concept", "title": "Religious plurality"}], "description": "The point is to understand conquest, adaptation, and new cultural formations without reducing them to conflict."}, {"id": "86dbb835-5d42-4f08-8093-9581e5214329", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Mughal Empire", "children": [{"id": "439f517c-d0c1-4f72-bfa2-b64ac39c534b", "type": "concept", "title": "Akbar and imperial ideology"}, {"id": "bd5df7d5-2060-4e2a-9c72-7d8fb40bce2e", "type": "concept", "title": "Mansabdari and revenue systems"}, {"id": "06b90108-06ca-47b7-b4a2-02b33d2af88f", "type": "concept", "title": "Art, architecture, and court culture"}], "description": "Students examine Mughal rule as a sophisticated imperial system with regional variation and contested authority."}, {"id": "df09c5a1-a0cf-453a-aa41-6ef89a5b18d1", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Indian Ocean South Asia", "children": [{"id": "e5be60df-0556-4566-be7c-bed569d395d6", "type": "concept", "title": "Port cities"}, {"id": "9446697c-d183-4c3f-a1f4-80b63c4d14af", "type": "concept", "title": "Merchants and credit"}, {"id": "c4053802-45cc-48b1-8486-955a08b8f378", "type": "concept", "title": "Connections to Arabia, East Africa, and Southeast Asia"}], "description": "This broadens the region through trade, migration, pilgrimage, and diaspora."}]}, {"id": "8a6391d8-70f1-4959-9e97-1c942c42a143", "type": "topic", "title": "Southeast Asia, Indian Ocean Worlds, and Maritime Exchange", "description": "Southeast Asia is treated as a historically creative crossroads rather than a passive meeting point. Students study how local societies selected, reshaped, and resisted outside influences.", "children": [{"id": "b7f4418b-0479-4992-98cd-75694e18342d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Mandala states and political culture", "children": [{"id": "8e6743e9-8569-4074-886a-bfd0a3572fd8", "type": "concept", "title": "Angkor"}, {"id": "1f12ad29-6ed8-42e2-b441-a20d8da671f9", "type": "concept", "title": "Srivijaya and Majapahit"}, {"id": "126776cb-f1a3-4b8c-8f06-0eed10ec3e53", "type": "concept", "title": "Mainland and island polities"}], "description": "Students learn models of power based on centers, networks, ritual authority, and shifting allegiance."}, {"id": "6c289b9b-8947-4d6f-b697-8df510f22a5c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Islamization and religious plurality", "children": [{"id": "2617dcb0-fc70-4079-945a-1f8c06487347", "type": "concept", "title": "Malay-Indonesian Islam"}, {"id": "394e1afd-23f1-4444-9ebc-57b7b9db6b05", "type": "concept", "title": "Buddhist kingdoms"}, {"id": "957924fe-a932-4e6d-96f2-14742dad830b", "type": "concept", "title": "Christian missions"}], "description": "The gist is to trace religious change through trade, scholarship, court patronage, and local practice."}, {"id": "529a5abc-b672-464c-ac37-f5c0f01fcbba", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Maritime commerce", "children": [{"id": "4bad3265-88b5-4d0a-a6fc-ecc8616f09c7", "type": "concept", "title": "Straits of Malacca"}, {"id": "f811f573-2f56-4525-a43e-1133037518b5", "type": "concept", "title": "Chinese and Indian merchants"}, {"id": "d0842725-a3bf-4b01-96d1-5d8c17143c63", "type": "concept", "title": "European trading companies"}], "description": "Students connect spices, textiles, silver, ceramics, and labor to state and social change."}, {"id": "2b97a52e-7f28-4d76-9939-8079cafe1f57", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Colonial Southeast Asia", "children": [{"id": "d1fdb30a-4e04-4090-b4e5-db55542bb5a8", "type": "concept", "title": "Plantation economies"}, {"id": "714ae01f-b0e4-4c35-a3c3-4cc5bac0697a", "type": "concept", "title": "Missionaries and education"}, {"id": "69d8b272-edd0-4e30-a598-aa3d14aa5217", "type": "concept", "title": "Anti-colonial movements"}], "description": "This subtopic prepares students to compare Dutch, British, French, Spanish, and American imperial projects."}]}, {"id": "e2a80827-9d48-4868-ad03-a7153d315eea", "type": "topic", "title": "Early Modern Asia: Ming-Qing, Tokugawa, Joseon, Safavid-Mughal-Ottoman Connections", "description": "The course uses early modernity as a comparative problem, not a European template. Students ask how Asian states managed population, economy, knowledge, military power, and cross-cultural contact.", "children": [{"id": "1fefebc0-3766-442d-b4d6-ef710222835c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Ming and Qing China", "children": [{"id": "edbd18b8-219d-4123-ae8d-413571ba5ea3", "type": "concept", "title": "Ming maritime policy and commerce"}, {"id": "24cb7bf2-8dec-48d6-9e7e-08d862e62c77", "type": "concept", "title": "Qing conquest and multiethnic empire"}, {"id": "d780ff03-2d95-49b2-bb5f-06c08ea99a25", "type": "concept", "title": "Population and silver"}], "description": "Students study dynastic rule, commercialization, frontier expansion, and social change."}, {"id": "20aee15d-6a56-4fa9-81ca-a58f0701609d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Tokugawa Japan", "children": [{"id": "7ddfffb8-ea48-4be0-b1ff-67d5c34d4ae6", "type": "concept", "title": "Bakuhan system"}, {"id": "d362d144-3471-459a-913c-c8e90b660784", "type": "concept", "title": "Samurai, peasants, artisans, merchants"}, {"id": "42f40278-4ea3-4724-b970-44b2fde87f26", "type": "concept", "title": "Rangaku and global knowledge"}], "description": "The point is to understand peace, hierarchy, urban culture, and controlled foreign relations."}, {"id": "58684b45-18a1-4710-9ed8-f8345f69986f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Joseon Korea", "children": [{"id": "f8ceb1c1-40f5-4890-b2b2-d5b59cb53b5b", "type": "concept", "title": "Yangban elite"}, {"id": "5fc9a556-2f50-480d-a8cf-c25570e60cd5", "type": "concept", "title": "Ritual and family order"}, {"id": "fe202a1b-da58-42a1-91d1-738d29ed8de0", "type": "concept", "title": "Tributary diplomacy"}], "description": "Students analyze Neo-Confucian governance, social hierarchy, diplomacy, and reform debates."}, {"id": "84670a54-d93a-43a7-a25c-81f240331a39", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Connected Islamic empires", "children": [{"id": "d9b94072-7dbe-4e43-b905-1109450dcd2c", "type": "concept", "title": "Gunpowder empires"}, {"id": "256915d1-0b61-4dd0-90e2-86cbd1755a51", "type": "concept", "title": "Law and religious authority"}, {"id": "b6b8ce56-5caa-4714-898c-a18a4256f157", "type": "concept", "title": "Artistic and commercial circulation"}], "description": "This compares Ottoman, Safavid, and Mughal worlds as linked early modern formations."}]}, {"id": "daefc8e9-b753-450d-ab4b-92f1129b2015", "type": "topic", "title": "Colonialism, Imperialism, and Unequal Treaties", "description": "This section focuses on coercion, collaboration, reform, resistance, and knowledge production. Students learn that imperialism was military and economic, but also legal, cultural, racial, and archival.", "children": [{"id": "e1baee42-9fba-4d11-8654-c2a06ea0eb65", "type": "subtopic", "title": "European imperial systems", "children": [{"id": "48a5ef3f-2903-4eff-bf29-bd92e221f388", "type": "concept", "title": "British India"}, {"id": "63d017e2-99aa-4570-b669-dfd3a1ba73df", "type": "concept", "title": "Dutch East Indies"}, {"id": "611267db-00db-4ae9-906b-7b3e3cd3c7ea", "type": "concept", "title": "French Indochina"}], "description": "Students compare direct rule, indirect rule, company rule, and settler or plantation economies."}, {"id": "30dc4532-c5f2-46d3-ab9f-396938b12602", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Unequal treaties and treaty ports", "children": [{"id": "5a82f7ac-927c-42c5-bfe6-503b84e1bd3d", "type": "concept", "title": "Opium Wars"}, {"id": "54fa89a6-fde1-42b8-bba6-dd5152b9478f", "type": "concept", "title": "Treaty-port society"}, {"id": "2747a29c-49f4-42c8-8291-f0a286375fbc", "type": "concept", "title": "Missionaries and reformers"}], "description": "The gist is to understand sovereignty under pressure through law, diplomacy, commerce, and extraterritoriality."}, {"id": "25ac475f-fdd9-4933-ad26-d71cdf41ec99", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Japanese imperialism", "children": [{"id": "cdad64c7-19a7-4bdb-a320-efc7aee3077f", "type": "concept", "title": "Meiji state-building"}, {"id": "b719295d-2e58-4a81-9d5b-4144b38cea1f", "type": "concept", "title": "Korea and Taiwan"}, {"id": "d0c67bf0-2444-4ea2-8e60-23a5385aab9d", "type": "concept", "title": "Manchuria and total war"}], "description": "Students examine Japan as both a target of imperial pressure and an imperial power."}, {"id": "c761e499-8991-4523-8ae4-ba1f0a8d7e67", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Colonial knowledge and race", "children": [{"id": "d4f154f1-be20-41ba-a794-58b3a49518f2", "type": "concept", "title": "Orientalism"}, {"id": "42c60cc1-77c6-4067-b315-b74a4f9b6d2b", "type": "concept", "title": "Caste and ethnicity under empire"}, {"id": "ae127f6c-263e-41cc-8ed2-a3fe2caa121d", "type": "concept", "title": "Colonial museums and archives"}], "description": "This subtopic shows how censuses, ethnography, law, and education created categories that shaped rule."}]}, {"id": "60170e15-0dbf-4875-a714-824b2cf5da11", "type": "topic", "title": "Reform, Revolution, Nationalism, and State-Building", "description": "Students examine how Asian intellectuals, activists, workers, peasants, soldiers, and elites answered the crisis of empire. The goal is to compare political imaginaries without assuming a single path to modernity.", "children": [{"id": "395c78d6-58a7-42bd-ba2e-a9e8662ead42", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Reform movements", "children": [{"id": "a2e21df4-e7b5-45b3-b54a-9878533e71c3", "type": "concept", "title": "Late Qing reforms"}, {"id": "4103224c-15c4-48d9-9b9f-9da4dd3a2ef1", "type": "concept", "title": "Meiji reforms"}, {"id": "d439e365-e446-4a3c-b43d-f3ac2de520c1", "type": "concept", "title": "Ottoman and South Asian reform currents"}], "description": "Students see reform as a contested mix of institutional change, moral renewal, and survival strategy."}, {"id": "e18ec079-c379-4173-8868-058d3c83cb71", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Nationalism and anti-colonial politics", "children": [{"id": "a02be1ca-f575-4394-a84a-268193f3f078", "type": "concept", "title": "Indian National Congress"}, {"id": "78b48606-e65a-4f10-8505-0666c77ef20e", "type": "concept", "title": "Chinese nationalism"}, {"id": "2f89a704-6867-4697-95ba-f2570a7729f2", "type": "concept", "title": "Vietnamese, Indonesian, and Korean movements"}], "description": "The point is to connect mass politics with print, education, language, religion, and organization."}, {"id": "cc0a82dd-4c4d-423b-9fb5-f3442334d1b9", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Revolutionary ideologies", "children": [{"id": "43088d13-9e1a-4aeb-949b-1873ab5b9fb2", "type": "concept", "title": "1911 Revolution"}, {"id": "c19def8b-b3f3-43c9-b7ad-2571b3634c91", "type": "concept", "title": "May Fourth"}, {"id": "735f6e74-ba9a-45d0-8d99-44cd92c09158", "type": "concept", "title": "Peasant and labor mobilization"}], "description": "Students compare liberal, socialist, communist, religious, and monarchist visions of political order."}, {"id": "2c8b90dc-734f-4869-9b95-5028f0952f00", "type": "subtopic", "title": "State-building and citizenship", "children": [{"id": "d9a88a60-bdcb-4f5f-89a1-1fa32d341996", "type": "concept", "title": "Constitutions"}, {"id": "d97b20ed-8678-4443-9e04-c67eb5d4622a", "type": "concept", "title": "Minorities and borders"}, {"id": "9d085de9-ec4a-451f-8c68-4d8796654ea8", "type": "concept", "title": "Education and national culture"}], "description": "This subtopic asks who belonged in new states and on what terms."}]}, {"id": "9518ae8e-906d-4a9e-8545-08605a5b8b8f", "type": "topic", "title": "War, Occupation, Partition, and Memory in the Twentieth Century", "description": "This section confronts traumatic histories with care and analytical rigor. Students learn how violence reshapes states and societies, and how memory politics continues long after formal conflict ends.", "children": [{"id": "ccd7b3e1-65b5-4511-9537-766bb9ada8a8", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Asia-Pacific War", "children": [{"id": "8598627c-7b8a-4d65-8383-53d3caf00d5d", "type": "concept", "title": "Japanese occupation"}, {"id": "8ad445ab-4146-4464-a592-13dcc10d7f79", "type": "concept", "title": "Nanjing and atrocity debates"}, {"id": "50201592-51ad-4031-a303-3dd601df70e3", "type": "concept", "title": "Comfort women and forced labor"}], "description": "Students study war as regional catastrophe, imperial project, occupation regime, and memory problem."}, {"id": "472dc506-8505-4dde-b750-d3b886ad336e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Civil war and revolution", "children": [{"id": "019db48f-e35d-4088-8c25-ce2ff2b5a269", "type": "concept", "title": "Chinese Civil War"}, {"id": "1e0265bf-9144-46e8-9c1b-0c9e56676eac", "type": "concept", "title": "Korean War"}, {"id": "5c92e2a4-f755-4467-a6fc-c22023186ebf", "type": "concept", "title": "Vietnamese revolutionary wars"}], "description": "The gist is to connect military conflict with social revolution and state formation."}, {"id": "ea869490-a8cf-412d-b337-b17d2a41cb5b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Partition and displacement", "children": [{"id": "9a95820f-e358-483f-ad91-9005b1740b8b", "type": "concept", "title": "India and Pakistan"}, {"id": "f189dd14-8468-4b03-9be6-0c2c082e8210", "type": "concept", "title": "Korea"}, {"id": "1f434729-b3e6-45ac-a4ef-cf54878eef69", "type": "concept", "title": "Palestine and wider Asian contexts"}], "description": "Students analyze partition as border-making, mass migration, violence, and long-term political inheritance."}, {"id": "8c18f889-0d9c-4438-b1ed-e701372017ee", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Memory and public history", "children": [{"id": "53447e42-ea36-4827-92ac-b498ae0aa927", "type": "concept", "title": "Museums and memorials"}, {"id": "b7dd92f9-df0f-499f-9a60-576ea5a6bd7a", "type": "concept", "title": "Textbook controversies"}, {"id": "cb6df8d7-d729-46dd-8991-d94dab04f2bc", "type": "concept", "title": "Transitional justice"}], "description": "This subtopic asks how societies remember, deny, commemorate, and litigate the past."}]}, {"id": "8d18ea3a-db80-4af3-9e49-9e3b8257d53f", "type": "topic", "title": "Communism, Cold War Asia, Developmental States, and Political Economy", "description": "The Cold War in Asia was not merely a U.S.-Soviet overlay. Students study local revolutions, regional wars, developmental ambitions, and global capitalism as intertwined historical processes.", "children": [{"id": "d8063ff8-a0b6-41a3-ba79-4927c0588757", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Socialist revolutions and regimes", "children": [{"id": "f0bb14ea-144c-4606-bf89-ab388cc7fb27", "type": "concept", "title": "People's Republic of China"}, {"id": "7115448b-dfb7-4ad1-9e97-9c00b931fcd4", "type": "concept", "title": "Vietnam"}, {"id": "f44852ac-ffb0-4acd-9880-2034c756d998", "type": "concept", "title": "Cambodia and Khmer Rouge"}], "description": "Students compare revolutionary legitimacy, land reform, party-state building, and social engineering."}, {"id": "d9dfa616-801a-4238-b2c8-60259dd4aa14", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Developmental states", "children": [{"id": "d5b2664a-e349-4dbe-9ade-082d19de366d", "type": "concept", "title": "Japan's postwar growth"}, {"id": "a14bb157-8f9c-43a7-9fca-ebcd4d737125", "type": "concept", "title": "South Korea and Taiwan"}, {"id": "904cff54-3740-4603-9801-72fa380bad7c", "type": "concept", "title": "Singapore and Southeast Asian models"}], "description": "The point is to understand rapid industrialization through state policy, labor discipline, capital, and geopolitics."}, {"id": "62d66785-674c-46c0-9d99-eebf58da00de", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Cold War security orders", "children": [{"id": "d6cd6889-3681-482b-a8e0-67847a48c4e1", "type": "concept", "title": "U.S. alliances"}, {"id": "ce250b93-b7ae-4fd3-a2f1-691e645976e9", "type": "concept", "title": "Nonalignment"}, {"id": "c10a4be1-322c-4987-ae8d-ac670ad563a7", "type": "concept", "title": "Insurgency and counterinsurgency"}], "description": "Students learn how alliances, intervention, and military regimes shaped domestic politics."}, {"id": "e4505074-e0b0-4855-8fd9-915c29e9ad3d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Reform, markets, and inequality", "children": [{"id": "6afe5133-12f4-44e7-b239-a2efd0963411", "type": "concept", "title": "China after 1978"}, {"id": "c0ee5cf6-6ec9-4349-b2ac-07ae7ae75c3f", "type": "concept", "title": "India after 1991"}, {"id": "dbc917bc-0387-4f3c-8a09-5d0c7bab8cb6", "type": "concept", "title": "Export economies and labor regimes"}], "description": "This subtopic links liberalization to migration, urbanization, environment, and social stratification."}]}, {"id": "bd33f90d-a027-46dd-b34f-513120d9458c", "type": "topic", "title": "Society, Gender, Labor, Migration, and Everyday Life", "description": "This section shifts from state narratives to lived history. Students learn how ordinary people made, endured, negotiated, and transformed large historical structures.", "children": [{"id": "05462723-fec8-415a-b4f6-4606c1647ec5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Gender and family", "children": [{"id": "e87c67e2-25c6-4b9c-aa8e-eb5ea593286f", "type": "concept", "title": "Patriarchy and household authority"}, {"id": "df46db46-dc5e-4956-9eba-0d6f80a45e6b", "type": "concept", "title": "Women in reform and revolution"}, {"id": "c54480bf-610f-41e4-93ae-721fc47f36dd", "type": "concept", "title": "Gender and empire"}], "description": "Students analyze law, labor, kinship, sexuality, reform, and feminist movements across periods and regions."}, {"id": "1432ceba-34ce-4dd1-8018-c91ee9c5ee5b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Labor and class", "children": [{"id": "392a27d7-dfe6-455e-8118-4626681c42bc", "type": "concept", "title": "Peasant labor"}, {"id": "62b1d92e-e10f-4754-a4b0-ecf42ef9ef38", "type": "concept", "title": "Industrial workers"}, {"id": "8d5fedce-45c9-4acb-9be3-9b91b0d56d6d", "type": "concept", "title": "Migrant and informal labor"}], "description": "The gist is to connect work to capitalism, empire, socialism, and development."}, {"id": "f719c930-74af-48bd-95d3-f0aa179382ba", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Migration and diaspora", "children": [{"id": "355527f0-ec62-4acd-bc9e-a2854c850cb7", "type": "concept", "title": "Chinese diaspora"}, {"id": "0f7650da-2bf4-4296-aed7-c423a9d43aa1", "type": "concept", "title": "South Asian diaspora"}, {"id": "0f758318-6cff-4c14-97bf-5420c6d6adf8", "type": "concept", "title": "Refugees and transnational families"}], "description": "Students trace Asian mobility across oceans, empires, borders, and labor systems."}, {"id": "e6f79e82-10ef-4527-9c5a-094421b631b9", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Urban and popular culture", "children": [{"id": "988885e2-83f4-41b0-ae24-688ccdefc3bd", "type": "concept", "title": "Cities and housing"}, {"id": "279ab484-95ab-4bf7-919e-54719c264b7f", "type": "concept", "title": "Film, music, and print culture"}, {"id": "6e24e97e-ed96-4d11-b519-48720bd6989c", "type": "concept", "title": "Consumption and youth cultures"}], "description": "This subtopic uses everyday life to understand modernity, identity, and social aspiration."}]}, {"id": "7d1e1de1-fa59-4212-8eee-2002f2b629b7", "type": "topic", "title": "Contemporary Asia: Globalization, Environment, Technology, and Geopolitics", "description": "The final substantive unit treats the present as history in motion. Students use historical thinking to analyze contemporary disputes, economic systems, environmental crises, and cultural change.", "children": [{"id": "0f628d42-8201-436a-be71-f6f94ce390d7", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Globalization and regional integration", "children": [{"id": "6ca11425-3263-4b5d-a8e0-df333269d6ed", "type": "concept", "title": "ASEAN and regional institutions"}, {"id": "45c85195-b777-48aa-90f1-b8595fb67172", "type": "concept", "title": "Belt and Road"}, {"id": "90487493-3c2a-451a-98cc-b467887c918f", "type": "concept", "title": "Global supply chains"}], "description": "Students examine production chains, trade, finance, tourism, and cultural exchange."}, {"id": "29c52ca4-1838-4824-a3cc-7bad3d152d91", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Environment and climate", "children": [{"id": "ed384838-5506-4c11-a8b7-f1dc24b42bf0", "type": "concept", "title": "Dams and rivers"}, {"id": "01b9949a-fce4-41e8-b3c2-a5d841d1a6c6", "type": "concept", "title": "Pollution and public health"}, {"id": "5e4443f1-c18e-43d6-9970-6aaffe5c6e9a", "type": "concept", "title": "Climate vulnerability and adaptation"}], "description": "The point is to study environmental change as political, economic, and social history."}, {"id": "d6adebea-e20b-40b7-a021-1f3f3b62506a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Technology and media", "children": [{"id": "e25d0a4b-dd15-47aa-87e2-afff3421981a", "type": "concept", "title": "Internet governance"}, {"id": "8e4a54be-8bc9-46e9-ac69-5c08bb819fa9", "type": "concept", "title": "K-pop, anime, Bollywood, and soft power"}, {"id": "d955415a-8e82-4e45-a3ac-c6106820e58c", "type": "concept", "title": "AI and labor futures"}], "description": "Students connect digital infrastructure, surveillance, platforms, and popular culture to historical change."}, {"id"